  "summary": "Screened all 52 clients with the all-client views of client_updates, client_performance, drift_by_round, feature_summary and subgroup_outcomes (Sex, AgeGroup, Income), then confirmed with per-client update views for WA, AZ and WI. The budget is fully spent (20.0 used).\n\nReported three clients (WA, AZ, WI) for explanation_instability from round 1. Each shows update norms several times the size-adjusted median and low, erratic cosine to the median update, while local AUC and CITL look normal. I chose explanation_instability over poisoning because the cosines are not consistently negative (sign-flip) or consistently aligned (scaled). WI is the least certain of the three.\n\nNot reported, as weak or unconfirmed leads:\n- GU: norm-ratio z=+4.9, CITL z=-3.9 with normal AUC, and a BMI drift from round 3. It is a small client (n=708), so the signals could be noise. I could not confirm them because the budget ran out.\n- CT: GenHlth tail z_noise=6.1 but no sentinel pattern in the mean or low percentiles, and normal local performance.\n- DC: DiffWalk drift z=+3.9 on a small client.\n- CA (AgeGroup 8) and a few tiny-subgroup outliers in subgroup_outcomes: these show high observed/expected ratios, the opposite of subgroup under-diagnosis, and are within noise.\n- No subgroup_bias signal (no low O/E with high FNR), and no label-noise AUC outliers.\n\nConfidence is moderate overall. The three update anomalies are the strongest and most consistent signal, but I could not check the per-round pattern against the other fault types with remaining budget."
